---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import MoreInCategory from "../components/MoreInCategory.astro";
import { authorSchema, authorName } from "../data/authors";
import AuthorAvatar from "../components/AuthorAvatar.astro";
import QuickPicks from "../components/QuickPicks.astro";
import PlanTheJob from "../components/PlanTheJob.astro";
import AuthorBio from "../components/AuthorBio.astro";
import { amazonLink } from "../data/affiliate";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";
import productImages from "../data/product-images.json";
import { pageDates } from "../data/dates";

const moreInCategory = [
  {
    "title": "How to Remove Old Caulk",
    "url": "/how-to-remove-old-caulk/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Painter's Tapes",
    "url": "/best-painters-tape/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Strip Paint From a Deck",
    "url": "/how-to-strip-paint-from-a-deck/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Concrete Floor Paint",
    "url": "/best-concrete-floor-paint/"
  },
  {
    "title": "How to Get Oil Out of Concrete",
    "url": "/how-to-get-oil-out-of-concrete/"
  },
  {
    "title": "Best Vinyl Plank Flooring",
    "url": "/best-vinyl-plank-flooring/"
  }
];
const dates = pageDates(Astro.url.pathname, { published: "2026-11-02", modified: "2026-11-02" });
const title = "7 Best Adhesive Removers in 2026: Stickers, Tape, Cars and Floors";
const description =
  "Seven adhesive removers compared by chemistry, safe surfaces and job: sticker and tape residue, car paint, labels, carpet glue and old black mastic on concrete";

const PRODUCTS = [
  {
    "id": "goo-gone-original",
    "name": "Goo Gone Original Adhesive Remover",
    "category": "Citrus-scented oil-based liquid",
    "badge": "Best Overall",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Maker lists carpet, finished wood, sealed stone, painted surfaces, glass, fabric, metal, plastic, ceramic and hard vinyl as safe",
      "Very effective on paper stickers, price tags, crayon and gum",
      "Clear do-not-use list printed by the maker",
      "Sold almost everywhere in small bottles"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Leaves an oily film that must be washed off",
      "Not for silk, leather, suede, rubber, drywall, unfinished wood or unsealed stone"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The right first bottle for stickers, labels and light tape residue around the house, as long as you wash the surface afterwards.",
    "description": "Goo Gone Original is the default household adhesive remover for good reason. It is a citrus-scented, oil-based liquid that soaks into sticker and tape adhesive and keeps it soft enough to wipe away, and the maker lists a wide range of safe surfaces: carpet, finished wood, sealed stone, painted surfaces, glass, fabric, metals, plastics, ceramic and porcelain, and hard vinyl. Directions are simple. Apply it, let it sit 5 to 10 minutes on extra gooey messes, wipe, then wash with hot soapy water to remove the oily film. On carpet and upholstery you blot it from a white cloth rather than pouring it on. What makes it a good overall pick is that Goo Gone is unusually specific about where not to use it: silk, leather, suede, rubber, faux stainless steel, drywall, unfinished wood and unsealed stone. Follow that list and pre-test, and it is very hard to go wrong. The label also warns that it contains petroleum distillates and is harmful or fatal if swallowed, so store it like any other household solvent. It is not strong enough for flooring mastic or cured construction adhesive, and it is too oily for jobs where you want the surface to dry clean, such as paper or car paint."
  },
  {
    "id": "goo-gone-pro-power",
    "name": "Goo Gone Pro-Power Adhesive Remover",
    "category": "Professional-strength liquid and spray gel",
    "badge": "Best for Tape Residue",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Stronger formula aimed at tape, glue and heavier residue",
      "Spray gel version clings to vertical surfaces",
      "Sold in sizes from 8 oz up to a gallon jug",
      "Same pre-test and exclusions approach as the Original"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Owners report it softens but does not dissolve cured caulk or roofing adhesive",
      "Oily formula can stain plywood, leather and fabric"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "Worth the step up when Original is too slow on old duct tape, foam tape or label glue that has been baking for years.",
    "description": "Pro-Power is Goo Gone's professional-strength formula, sold as a pourable liquid in 8 ounce, 32 ounce and gallon sizes and as a 24 ounce spray gel. It targets the jobs where Original runs out of steam: duct tape and carpet tape residue, foam mounting tape, heavy label glue and sticky buildup that has been on a surface for years. The spray gel is the version to buy for walls, doors and car glass, because it clings instead of running down the surface before it has time to work. Like Original, the label tells you to pre-test on an inconspicuous area and not to use it on silk, leather, suede or rubber. Treat it as the same chemistry with more bite rather than a different kind of product: it is still oily, it still needs a soapy wash afterwards, and one owner warns it may stain plywood, leather and most fabrics. Owner reports are also a useful reality check on its limits. Several buyers found it softened caulk and roofing adhesive without dissolving them, which is typical of any liquid remover on cured sealants. For thick, aged tape residue on hard, sealed surfaces, though, it saves a lot of scraping."
  },
  {
    "id": "3m-general-purpose-adhesive-cleaner",
    "name": "3M General Purpose Adhesive Cleaner 08984",
    "category": "Automotive solvent cleaner",
    "badge": "Best for Cars",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "3M states it will not harm most thoroughly cured automotive paint, vinyl or fabric when used properly",
      "Removes wax, grease, oil, tar and adhesive residue",
      "Evaporates rather than leaving an oily film",
      "Long-standing body shop standard"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Flammable solvent, labeled for professional use",
      "Not for fresh or uncured paint, and not VOC-compliant in some areas"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The remover to use on a car: badges, decals, pinstripes, tar and dealer stickers, on fully cured paint only.",
    "description": "3M General Purpose Adhesive Cleaner, part number 08984, is a solvent cleaner sold in a 1 quart can to body shops and detailers, and it is the safest way we know of to take adhesive off a car's paint. 3M describes it as quickly removing waxes, greases, oil, tar and adhesive residue, and says it will not harm most thoroughly cured automotive paint, vinyls or fabrics when properly used. Because it is a thin, volatile solvent rather than an oil, it flashes off and leaves the panel clean, which matters when you plan to wax or reapply a decal. The qualifiers in that safety claim are real. The technical data sheet cautions against use on fresh or uncured paint, so a recent respray should be left alone, and the product is flammable and labeled for professional use. 3M and its distributors also note that VOC limits vary by state and locality, and that 3M offers its 38984 Specialty Adhesive Remover as a more compliant alternative where 08984 is restricted. Apply it to a towel, not the panel, use plastic tools, then wash and wax. At home it also cleans tar from boat hulls and adhesive from glass, but it is overkill for a price tag on a mug."
  },
  {
    "id": "un-du-original",
    "name": "Un-Du Original Formula Adhesive Remover",
    "category": "Fast-evaporating solvent with scraper",
    "badge": "Best for Labels and Paper",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Attached scraper on the 4 oz bottle",
      "Maker lists glass, plastic, fiberglass, painted finishes, carpet, upholstery and finished leather",
      "Original formula marketed as photo-safe and acid-free",
      "Evaporates without an oily residue"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Highly flammable; Original not sold in California",
      "Hard on skin and nails, so wear gloves"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The specialist for labels on books, jars, glass and framed work, where an oily remover would leave a stain.",
    "description": "Un-Du is a fast-evaporating solvent remover sold in a 4 ounce bottle with a flat scraper built into the cap and in a larger commercial size used by framers. You drip it onto or under a label, it penetrates the adhesive in seconds, and the scraper lifts the label off whole. Because it evaporates rather than leaving oil behind, the adhesive can often be reused on the label, and the surface is left dry. The maker and retailers list glass, plastic, fiberglass, painted finishes, upholstery, carpet and finished leather as compatible, along with hot melt glue, window decals and vinyl lettering, and the Original Formula is marketed as photo-safe, acid-free and archival-safe, which is why libraries and booksellers stock it. Even so, conservation supplier Talas recommends testing on each new material first. Two cautions. The Original Formula is highly flammable and cannot be sold in California, where a VOC-compliant version is sold instead. And owners note it is tough on fingernails and skin, so gloves are sensible for anything more than one label. It is not the tool for big tape jobs or floors, and stubborn residues may need more than one application."
  },
  {
    "id": "motsenbockers-lift-off",
    "name": "Motsenbocker's Lift Off Tape, Label and Adhesive Remover",
    "category": "Water-based remover",
    "badge": "Best Low-Odor",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Water-based formula with low odor",
      "Rated for duct tape, painter's tape, glue, stickers and labels",
      "Unscented version available",
      "Gentlest option for walls and indoor rooms"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Slower than solvents on old, baked-on residue",
      "Some retail listings describe the formula inconsistently, so check the label"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The one to reach for indoors, around kids and on painted walls, when you would rather wait a little longer than breathe solvent.",
    "description": "Motsenbocker's Lift Off Tape, Label and Adhesive Remover takes a different route from the citrus and solvent products. Retail listings describe a patented water-based formula that loosens the bond between the residue and the surface rather than dissolving the adhesive in oil or solvent, and Lowe's lists it for duct tape, painter's tape, glue, stickers and labels. It comes in a 22 ounce trigger spray, a smaller squeeze bottle and an unscented version, which Staples notes was named a best new formulation by Popular Science. The case for it is comfort and caution. It is the lowest-odor product here, it does not leave a slick oily film, and it is the sensible first try on painted walls, nursery furniture, school supplies and anything else where fumes or a dark oil mark would be a problem. The case against is speed. On old duct tape or foam tape that has cured in the sun, owners commonly report needing longer dwell times and repeat applications, and a solvent will be quicker. One retailer page even lists the product as solvent-based in its specification table while calling it water-based in the description, so read the label on the bottle you buy. Pre-test on flat paint as you would with anything else."
  },
  {
    "id": "sentinel-747-plus",
    "name": "Sentinel 747 Plus Adhesive Remover",
    "category": "Flooring mastic and carpet glue remover",
    "badge": "Best for Flooring Adhesive",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Rated for adhesive left by vinyl tile, sheet vinyl, carpet and wood flooring",
      "100 to 200 sq ft per gallon coverage",
      "10 to 20 minute dwell, much faster than soy removers",
      "Low odor, sold in gallons and pails"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Requires a thorough wash and rinse before new flooring",
      "Not a substitute for asbestos testing on black mastic"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The practical gallon for a homeowner pulling up carpet or vinyl tile and facing a floor of old glue, once asbestos is ruled out.",
    "description": "Sentinel is a long-established name in floor abatement chemicals, and 747 Plus is the version most homeowners find on the shelf, sold in a 128 ounce jug at home centers and in larger pails through flooring suppliers. The maker rates it for adhesives left behind after removing vinyl tile, sheet vinyl, indoor and outdoor carpet, and wood flooring, with coverage of 100 to 200 square feet per gallon depending on how thick the old adhesive is. You spray or mop it on, let it soak 10 to 20 minutes until the mastic is saturated, then scrape or squeegee the softened adhesive into piles and contain it with absorbent. Distributors describe it as low odor and biodegradable, and one lists its VOC content at no more than 15 grams per liter. The part people skip is cleanup. Sentinel specifies washing and rinsing the floor with its 810 cleaner and water and letting it dry at least 24 hours before new flooring goes down, because remover residue interferes with new adhesive. Protect walls and drains while you work. If the adhesive is black and the house is old enough, test it for asbestos first; 747 Plus makes the work wet and scrapeable, but it does not make asbestos safe."
  },
  {
    "id": "blue-bear-500mr",
    "name": "Blue Bear BEAN-e-doo 500MR Mastic Remover",
    "category": "Soy-based mastic remover",
    "badge": "Best Soy-Based",
    "amazonUrl": "#",
    "pros": [
      "Soy-based, low VOC and low odor",
      "Rated for black mastic, carpet and tile adhesive, and layered old glues",
      "Clear guidance on asbestos limits",
      "Sold in quarts for small areas and gallons for rooms"
    ],
    "cons": [
      "Slow: 1 to 2 hours for black mastic, 4 to 8 for vinyl and acrylic",
      "Not for wood, and can darken some concrete"
    ],
    "bottomLine": "The low-odor choice for black mastic and layered adhesive on concrete, if you have the patience to let it work.",
    "description": "Blue Bear 500MR is a soy-based mastic remover made from soybeans instead of petroleum distillates, sold in quarts and gallons. The maker rates it for carpet and tile adhesives, vinyl and acrylic mastics, black mastic, layered adhesives and older flooring glues on concrete, and it is low odor enough to be used in occupied schools and hospitals. The catch is time. Blue Bear says black and asbestos-containing mastic typically softens in 1 to 2 hours, while vinyl and acrylic mastics usually need 4 to 8 hours, and the age, thickness, number of layers and temperature all change that. A quart covers 25 to 50 square feet and a gallon 100 to 200. Once softened, you scrape with a long-handled floor scraper or squeegee, clean the residue with a degreaser or soap and water, and rinse until rubbing the wet floor no longer produces cloudy white water. It is not for wood subfloors, some concrete may darken, and you should protect drywall and drains. We like that the maker is direct about asbestos: 500MR can soften asbestos-containing mastic, but it does not identify asbestos or replace testing, and confirmed asbestos mastic should be handled by trained professionals. Owner reports on carpet glue are mixed."
  }
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 1,
          name: "Home",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 2,
          name: "Best Lists",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-of/",
        },
        {
          "@type": "ListItem",
          position: 3,
          name: "Best Adhesive Removers",
          item: "https://www.thehonestreviewers.com/best-adhesive-remover/",
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "What is the best adhesive remover for sticker residue?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For most sticker residue on glass, plastic, finished wood and painted metal, Goo Gone Original is the simplest choice. Peel off as much paper as you can, apply the remover, let it sit for a few minutes on stubborn spots, wipe with a plastic scraper and wash with soapy water. On paper, books or framed glass, Un-Du is better because it evaporates instead of leaving oil."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Is Goo Gone safe on car paint?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Goo Gone sells automotive-specific versions, and its Original lists painted surfaces as safe, but on a car we prefer a product made and labeled for automotive paint. 3M General Purpose Adhesive Cleaner 08984 is labeled as not harming most thoroughly cured automotive paint when used properly. Whatever you use, avoid fresh paint, apply to a towel, use plastic tools, then wash and wax."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Will adhesive remover damage painted walls?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "It can. Flat and matte latex paint is soft and porous, and oily removers can leave a darker, shinier patch. Start with warm soapy water or a water-based remover such as Motsenbocker's Lift Off, test in a hidden spot, work only on the residue and wipe with a damp cloth. Goo Gone lists drywall among the surfaces not to use it on."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do you remove black mastic from concrete?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "First, have the mastic tested for asbestos, because some old asphaltic cutback adhesive contained it. If it tests negative, apply a mastic remover such as Sentinel 747 Plus or a soy remover such as Blue Bear 500MR, let it soak for the labeled time, scrape it wet with a floor scraper, then wash and rinse the slab thoroughly before installing new flooring. Never sand or grind it."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Are soy-based adhesive removers as good as solvents?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "They can be just as effective on flooring mastic, but they are much slower. Blue Bear says black mastic typically softens in 1 to 2 hours and vinyl or acrylic mastic in 4 to 8, while a product like Sentinel 747 Plus calls for 10 to 20 minutes. Soy removers trade speed for low odor and low VOCs, which matters in an occupied home."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "Can I use WD-40 or rubbing alcohol instead?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "For small jobs, often yes. Rubbing alcohol works on many fresh label adhesives on glass and hard plastic and evaporates cleanly, and light oils soften sticker gum much like citrus removers do. Both have the same limits: test first, keep oils off porous surfaces, wash afterwards, and do not expect either to touch flooring mastic or cured construction adhesive."
          }
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          "name": "How do I get dried construction adhesive off a subfloor?",
          "acceptedAnswer": {
            "@type": "Answer",
            "text": "Mostly by scraping. Cured construction adhesive does not dissolve in household removers, so cut ridges down with a sharp floor scraper, a heat gun or an oscillating tool fitted with a scraper blade, then sand only if the adhesive is a modern product you know is asbestos-free. Uncured adhesive cleans up with the solvent on its label, usually mineral spirits, so wipe drips immediately."
          }
        }
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: authorSchema(Astro.url.pathname),
      datePublished: dates.published,
      dateModified: dates.modified,
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "The Honest Reviewers" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "ItemList",
      name: title,
      numberOfItems: PRODUCTS.length,
      itemListElement: PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => ({
        "@type": "ListItem",
        position: index + 1,
        name: product.name,
        url: new URL(`#${product.id}`, Astro.url).toString(),
      })),
    },
    ...PRODUCTS.map((product) => ({
      "@type": "Product",
      name: product.name,
      description: product.description,
      brand: {
        "@type": "Brand",
        name: product.name.split(" ")[0],
      },
    })),
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: authorName(Astro.url.pathname),
    section: "Home Improvement",
    tags: [
      "best adhesive remover",
      "adhesive remover",
      "sticker residue remover",
      "goo gone",
      "mastic remover",
      "tape residue"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="bg-gray-900 py-16 lg:py-24 relative overflow-hidden">
      <div class="absolute inset-0 opacity-10" style="background-image: radial-gradient(circle at 20% 20%, #38BDF8 0, transparent 45%), radial-gradient(circle at 80% 60%, #FF4500 0, transparent 40%);"></div>
      <div class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 relative z-10 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex justify-center gap-3 mb-6">
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-[#FF4500]/20 text-[#FF4500] text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest border border-[#FF4500]/30">
            Buyer's Guide
          </span>
          <span class="inline-flex items-center gap-1.5 px-3 py-1.5 rounded-full bg-green-500/10 text-green-400 text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest border border-green-500/20">
            7 Products Compared
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight mb-8">
          The 7 Best <span class="text-[#38BDF8]">Adhesive Removers</span>
        </h1>
        <p class="text-xl text-gray-300 leading-relaxed mb-8 max-w-3xl mx-auto">
          Citrus, solvent, water-based and soy removers matched to the job: stickers, tape, car badges, labels, carpet glue and flooring mastic, with the surfaces each one can damage.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-col sm:flex-row gap-4 justify-center">
          <a
            href="#goo-gone-original"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-[#FF4500] hover:bg-[#E63E00] text-white font-black rounded-xl shadow-lg transition-all no-underline"
          >
            See Our #1 Pick
          </a>
          <a
            href="#top-picks"
            class="px-8 py-4 bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white font-bold rounded-xl border border-white/10 no-underline"
          >
            How We Picked
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Author & Meta -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pt-12">
      <div class="flex items-center gap-6 border-b border-gray-100 pb-8">
        <div class="flex items-center gap-3">
          <AuthorAvatar size={48} />
          <div>
            <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Shaheer Ali Khan</p>
            <p class="text-xs text-gray-500 uppercase tracking-widest">Consumer Product Testing &amp; Materials Performance Specialist</p>
          </div>
        </div>
        <div class="h-10 w-px bg-gray-200 hidden sm:block"></div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block">
          <p class="text-sm font-bold text-gray-900">Last Updated</p>
          <p class="text-xs text-gray-500">{dates.display}</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="7.9k" />
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Table of Contents -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
      <div class="bg-gray-50 border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8">
        <h2 class="text-lg font-black text-gray-900 mb-4 flex items-center gap-2">
          In This Guide
        </h2>
        <nav class="grid grid-cols-1 sm:grid-cols-2 gap-2">
          <a href="#chemistries" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">1. Four Chemistries</a>
          <a href="#how-we-picked" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">2. How We Picked</a>
          <a href="#top-picks" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">3. Our Top Picks</a>
          <a href="#match-the-job" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">4. Match Remover to Residue</a>
          <a href="#surfaces" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">5. Surface Safety</a>
          <a href="#cars" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">6. Adhesive on Cars</a>
          <a href="#flooring" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">7. Flooring Mastic and Carpet Glue</a>
          <a href="#asbestos" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">8. Black Mastic and Asbestos</a>
          <a href="#construction" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">9. Construction Adhesive and Caulk</a>
          <a href="#ventilation" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">10. Ventilation and Cleanup</a>
          <a href="#mistakes" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">11. Common Mistakes</a>
          <a href="#faq" class="text-sm text-gray-600 hover:text-[#1E90FF] no-underline py-1 font-medium">12. FAQ</a>
        </nav>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="text-2xl font-medium text-gray-900 mb-8 italic">
          The best adhesive remover for most homes is Goo Gone Original, a citrus-scented, oil-based liquid that handles stickers, labels and light tape residue on most sealed surfaces. For cars, use 3M General Purpose Adhesive Cleaner on fully cured paint. For labels on paper and glass, use Un-Du. For floors, buy a gallon of mastic remover such as Sentinel 747 Plus, and if the glue is old black mastic, have it tested for asbestos before you scrape, and never sand it.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="chemistries" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Citrus, Solvent, Water-Based or Soy: Four Chemistries
          </h2>

          <p>
            Citrus and oil-based removers are the household default. Goo Gone is the best-known example, and it is worth knowing that the citrus is mostly the scent: the Original label warns that it contains petroleum distillates and is harmful or fatal if swallowed. These products are oily on purpose. The oil dissolves into sticker and tape adhesive and keeps it soft, which is why they work well on label gum, crayon and price tags, and why they leave a greasy film you must wash off with hot soapy water afterwards.
          </p>

          <p>
            Fast solvent removers, such as 3M General Purpose Adhesive Cleaner and Un-Du, use thin, volatile solvents that cut adhesive quickly and then evaporate instead of leaving an oily layer. That makes them the better choice for cars, paper, glass and anything you want clean rather than slick. The trade-off is flammability and fumes. 3M describes its cleaner as solvent based and for professional use, and Un-Du's original formula is labeled highly flammable and is not sold in California, where a VOC-compliant version replaces it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Water-based removers like Motsenbocker's Lift Off swap the oil and solvent for a water-based formula that loosens the bond between the residue and the surface. They are the gentlest and lowest-odor option and the one to use in a child's room or on a surface you are nervous about, but they are slower on old, baked-on tape. Soy-based removers sit at the far end: methyl soyate and similar bio-based liquids built for flooring mastic. They are low odor and slow, measured in hours rather than minutes, and they are made for floors, not for stickers on a laptop.
          </p>

