        <div id="match-the-job" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. Match the Remover to the Residue
          </h2>

          <p>
            Paper stickers and price tags are the easy case. The paper face blocks the remover, so peel or scratch off as much of the paper as you can first, then soak what is left. Goo Gone's own directions allow 5 to 10 minutes for extra gooey messes before wiping. Un-Du goes on, flashes into the adhesive in under a minute and its attached scraper lifts the label, which is why framers and booksellers use it.
          </p>

          <p>
            Tape residue depends on the tape. Fresh masking or painter's tape lifts with almost anything, which is one reason a decent <a href="/best-painters-tape/" class="text-[#1E90FF] font-bold hover:underline">painter's tape</a> that releases cleanly is cheaper than any remover. Duct tape and foam mounting tape that have baked in a window or on a hot car leave a thick rubber-based layer that needs a longer soak with Pro-Power or a solvent, plus a plastic scraper. Vinyl decals and pinstriping want gentle heat first to soften the vinyl, then a remover for the glue left behind.
          </p>

          <p>
            Flooring adhesives are a separate world. Carpet glue, vinyl tile mastic and black cutback adhesive cover hundreds of square feet in a layer that may be decades old, and you need a gallon of a purpose-made mastic remover, a long dwell time and a floor scraper, not a squeeze bottle. Construction adhesive, once cured, barely responds to removers at all. The table below is a fast way to match the job.
          </p>

